From a3ef13ebe530c64fc743d5eb7cf75f1dce97435a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 05:04:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: BCM5301X: RT-N18U: set WAN MAC from nvram

The WAN port's MAC address is et0macaddr + 1, which user space sets on
the port today. Read it from nvram instead, as the RT-AC3100 does since
commit 00f8f89f3981 ("ARM: dts: BCM5301X: AC3100: set WAN MAC from
nvram"). The et0macaddr cell is the one that patch 376 ("ARM: dts:
BCM5301X: use IMP0 port 8 as the CPU port") adds to the RT-N18U device
tree for gmac2; the RT-AC3100 change brought a cell of its own.

The port then has an address of its own from the start, so it keeps it
when it moves to another CPU port; a port without one takes the
address of its new conduit, which on this board is the LAN address.

The value is the one OpenWrt sets from user space when nvram has no
wan_hwaddr, so it does not change.

Measured on an RT-N18U with this change: the WAN port's permanent
address was et0macaddr + 1, the port kept that address while it moved
to the other CPU port and back, and a download through it went on
across both moves.

Signed-off-by: Semih Baskan <strst.gs@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm47081-asus-rt-n18u.dts | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm47081-asus-rt-n18u.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/broadcom/bcm47081-asus-rt-n18u.dts
@@ -115,6 +115,9 @@
 	ports {
 		port@0 {
 			label = "wan";
+
+			nvmem-cells = <&et0macaddr 1>;
+			nvmem-cell-names = "mac-address";
 		};
 
 		port@1 {
